Issues needs write because you're configuring github-app globally under safe-outputs, so add-comment mints from the App rather than GITHUB_TOKEN. And Issues read specifically is required for update-project to resolve issue-backed project items — covered by the above. 
github

Then: Generate a private key (downloads a .pem), note the Client ID (Iv23..., not the numeric App ID), and Install App on the org, granting it the repo(s) you'll run from.

3. Wire up secrets
bash
gh variable set APP_CLIENT_ID --body "Iv23xxxxxxxx"
gh aw secrets set APP_PRIVATE_KEY --value "$(cat ~/Downloads/your-app.private-key.pem)"

For the Copilot engine: create a fine-grained PAT with resource owner = your user account (not the org) and Account permissions → Copilot Requests: Read, on an account with an active Copilot license. 
GitHub

bash
gh aw secrets set COPILOT_GITHUB_TOKEN --value "github_pat_..."
gh aw secrets bootstrap   # verifies what's set
4. Init the repo
bash
gh aw init
git add .gitattributes .github .vscode
git commit -m "Initialize gh-aw"
5. The workflow

.github/workflows/project-triage.md:

yaml
---
on:
  issues:
    types: [opened]

engine: copilot

permissions:
  contents: read
  issues: read
  actions: read

runs-on: [self-hosted, linux, x64]
runs-on-slim: [self-hosted, linux, x64]

tools:
  github:
    toolsets: [default, projects]
    github-app:
      client-id: ${{ vars.APP_CLIENT_ID }}
      private-key: ${{ secrets.APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}
      owner: "YOUR-ORG"
      repositories: ["*"]

safe-outputs:
  github-app:
    client-id: ${{ vars.APP_CLIENT_ID }}
    private-key: ${{ secrets.APP_PRIVATE_KEY }}
    owner: "YOUR-ORG"
  update-project:
    project: "https://github.com/orgs/YOUR-ORG/projects/1"
    max: 1
  add-comment:
    max: 1
---

# Project Triage

A new issue was opened. Read it, then add it to the project board and set its fields.

Always include the full project URL in the `update_project` call:
`https://github.com/orgs/YOUR-ORG/projects/1`

Set fields only from these exact values — never invent new ones:

- **Status**: `Needs Triage` | `Ready` | `In Progress` | `Blocked`
- **Priority**: `P0` | `P1` | `P2`
- **Team**: `Platform` | `Infra` | `Tooling`

New issues start at `Needs Triage` unless the report is complete enough to be
actionable, in which case use `Ready`. Reserve `P0` for production impact.

Then post one short comment explaining the routing and flagging any uncertainty.

If for some reason no action is appropriate, you MUST call the `noop` tool:
`{"noop": {"message": "No action needed: [reason]"}}`

Two details in there that are not optional: the agent's output must explicitly carry the project field — the value in frontmatter is documentation only, and if the agent finishes without calling any safe-output tool the workflow fails silently, which the docs call the #1 runtime failure mode for safe-output workflows. Hence spelling out noop in the prompt. 
